      "text": "To convert from rectangular coordinates $(x, y, z)$ to spherical coordinates $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$, we use the following formulas:\n\n1. $\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}$\n2. $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)$ if $x > 0$, and $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) + \\pi$ if $x < 0$ and $y \\ge 0$; otherwise, $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) - \\pi$ if $x < 0$ and $y < 0$; and finally, $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ if $x = 0$ and $y > 0$, $\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ if $x = 0$ and $y < 0$, and $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ if $x = 0$ and $y = 0$.\n3. $\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)$ if $\\rho \\neq 0$, $\\phi = 0$ if $\\rho = 0$.\n\nGiven the point $(0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)$, we can convert it to spherical coordinates as follows:\n\n1. Calculate $\\rho$:\n   \\[\n   \\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate $\\theta$:\n   Since $x = 0$ and $y = -3\\sqrt{3}$, we have $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)$. However, since $y < 0$ and $x = 0$, the point lies on the negative $y$-axis. Therefore, $\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\n3. Calculate $\\phi$:\n   \\[\n   \\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n   \\]\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(6, \\frac{3\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{3})}\n\\]",
