      "text": "To convert the point \\((0, -3\\sqrt{3}, 3)\\) from rectangular coordinates to spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\), we use the following formulas:\n\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right)\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate each component step by step.\n\n1. **Calculate \\(\\rho\\):**\n\\[\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3\\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{0 + 27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6\n\\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\theta\\):**\n\\[\n\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right)\n\\]\nSince \\(\\theta\\) is in the third quadrant (both \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are negative), and \\(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\) is undefined, we need to adjust our approach. Instead, we recognize that \\(\\theta\\) is directly given by the ratio of \\(y\\) to \\(x\\) in the third quadrant:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\pi + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\pi + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}}{0}\\right) = \\pi + \\frac{3\\pi}{3} = \\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{4\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\n3. **Calculate \\(\\phi\\):**\n\\[\n\\phi = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{3}{6}\\right) = \\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the spherical coordinates are:\n\\[\n(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left(6, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left(6, \\frac{4\\pi}{3}, \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}\n\\]",
